When considering whether to grant an injunction in cases involving the publication of private information about a plaintiff, courts are required to take into account several factors under s 6(1) of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth). These include:

- The nature and extent of the privacy invasion;
- Whether the publication is likely to cause distress or harm to the plaintiff;
- The likelihood that the publication will continue, recur, or be repeated;
- Any steps taken by the defendant to prevent further publication;
- The public interest in publishing the information.

The court must also consider whether granting an injunction would serve a compelling purpose and balance against any potential harm. [Source: s 6]
